olor:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Good morning, wo=
rld.</span><span> Last week=E2=80=99s midterm elections were perhaps the fi=
rst major elections fought at least in part over issues related to artifici=
al intelligence. This year saw record donations from tech executives to tec=
h-friendly candidates. And in some cases, A.I. was actually on the ballot: =
In local ballot initiatives across the country, people voted to limit or pr=
event the development of data centers. Many newly elected Democrats have pr=
omised A.I. regulation.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia=
,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>If A.I. has surged to the forefron=
t of U.S. politics, it=E2=80=99s in part because A.I. is at the forefront o=
f the U.S. economy. And stock markets around the world rise and fall with A=
=2EI. shares, particularly the multitrillion-dollar behemoths based in Sili=
con Valley. I spoke to my colleague Joe Rennison, who covers financial mark=
ets, about the dramatic swings in tech stocks following the midterms and wh=
at they mean =E2=80=94 for Americans, and for the rest of us.</span></p><p =
