/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:=
700">On regulation, voters seem to want it. But is the U.S. economy now so =
dependent on a handful of A.I. companies that even Democrats will have to t=
hink carefully about regulating A.I.=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 1=
7px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>I think that depen=
dency is a real concern. Just 10 companies make up roughly 40 percent of th=
e S&P 500, and so swings in the share prices of just a handful of big tech =
companies can move the entire market.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17p=
x/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But it=E2=80=99s wor=
th pointing out that most people who talk about more A.I. regulation or mor=
e scrutiny on A.I. tend to talk in terms of slowing down the industry, not =
stopping it entirely. Some analysts have actually argued that easing the ra=
pid pace of growth would alleviate some of the strains on the American comp=
anies driving that growth, and would boost profits and bolster their positi=
ons as industry leaders. In other words, a more gradual pace of growth coul=
d be healthier in the long run for these companies and for the whole econom=
y, even if it causes some short-term market pain.</span></p><p style=3D"fon=
t:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D=
"font-weight:700">How does the upcoming Anthropic I.P.O. fit into all this=
=3F It=E2=80=99s another blockbuster. What does its target valuation tell u=
s=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;m=
argin:0 0 15px"><span>Well, bankers had floated a $2 trillion valuation at =
one point; ultimately, Anthropic looks to be aiming for just (!) $1.4 trill=
ion, at least for now. So it seems like some of the air has come out over t=
he past couple of months, and that reflects a broader revaluation of public=
 companies. But it=E2=80=99s still a highly sought-after I.P.O., with plent=
y of investor demand. That=E2=80=99s important because its size means its p=
ublic debut is not just important to the A.I. industry but to the entire ma=
rket.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;=
margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">And what about for the res=
t of the world=3F Other markets have also had a bumpy ride the last few wee=
ks, right=3F How much do we all depend on U.S. tech companies, and U.S. pol=
itics=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#3=
33;margin:0 0 15px"><span>There=E2=80=99s no shortage of examples of the U.=
S. economy and financial markets driving developments around the world. But=
 in the case of A.I., it=E2=80=99s not just U.S. markets affecting the rest=
 of the world, but also vice versa.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/=
25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Japanese markets, beca=
use of low interest rates, have become a favorite place for investors to bo=
rrow money, which is then reinvested in U.S. stocks. Taiwan has the largest=
 manufacturer of chips designed by U.S. tech behemoth Nvidia. And South Kor=
ean stock markets have become home to highly leveraged bets on baskets of A=
=2EI. companies.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;=
color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>South Korea=E2=80=99s Kospi index fell ov=
